\label{sec:layers}

\begin{figure}[t]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.95\linewidth]{figures/fig5_layer_sweep.pdf}
    \caption{{\bf Layer sweep} (layers 4--24 of 32; dotted line marks the pre-registered layer 12). Left and middle: on contrasts that differ in prompt, the \repe and \instr probes reach 0.93--0.97 from layer 8 onward. Right: on the same-prompt lie vs.\ \pufalse contrast (stratified), no probe exceeds 0.62 at any layer, and the \correct probe stays at or below 0.50.}
    \label{fig:layers}
\end{figure}

The near-chance result is not an artefact of the fixed layer. \Figref{fig:layers} repeats the probe analysis at layers 4--24. On the standard contrasts, the \repe probe peaks at 0.97 (lie vs.\ \honestc) and 0.95 (lie vs.\ hallucination) between layers 10 and 24, so layer 12 is, if anything, slightly conservative. On the same-prompt lie vs.\ \pufalse contrast, the best value over all layers is 0.60 for \repe (layer 20), 0.61 for \truthp (layer 10), 0.62 for \instr (layer 16), and 0.50 for \correct (layer 12). The \repe probe on lie vs.\ \honestp ranges from 0.41 to 0.59 from layer 6 onward.

\subsection{\mask cross-check}
\label{sec:mask}

\input{tables/mask}

On \mask's known-facts and disinformation splits, which use realistic, implicit pressure on binary propositions, the pattern repeats (\tabref{tab:mask}). Of 334 pressured statements, 67 are lies, 23 honest, 55 false without a consistent belief, 34 true without a belief, 150 evasions, and 5 cases with a wrong belief. Lie detectors separate lies from honest answers in the same scenario (\repe 0.71, \instr 0.79) but not from false answers where the model holds no consistent belief (\repe 0.42 [0.32, 0.52], \instr 0.53). The mean-pooled truth probe is the only detector with a CI above chance on this contrast (0.69). The samples are small and the intervals are wide, but the direction agrees with \triviaqa.

